export const DATA_33: ReadingTest[] = [
  { id: "rd-040", num: 40, title: "Đề 040", timeMin: 60, parts: [
    { title: "The Popularity of Fast Food", paragraphs: ["Fast food has become one of the most recognizable features of modern life. Millions of people around the world eat at fast food restaurants every single day, choosing burgers, fried chicken, pizza, and sandwiches as their regular meals. Whether in large cities or small towns, fast food outlets can be found on almost every street corner, and their bright signs and familiar logos have become a common part of the landscape. But why has fast food become so popular, and what does this tell us about the way people live today?", "One of the biggest reasons for the success of fast food is speed and convenience. In today's busy world, many people simply do not have enough time to prepare and cook a full meal at home. Working adults, students, and families with young children often find themselves looking for something quick and easy to eat between work, school, and other activities. Fast food restaurants offer a solution to this problem by providing ready-made meals that can be ordered and received within minutes. Many outlets also offer drive-through services, so customers do not even need to leave their cars. This level of convenience is very difficult to match with home cooking.", "Another important factor is price. Fast food is generally much cheaper than eating at a sit-down restaurant, and in many countries it is also less expensive than buying fresh ingredients to cook at home. For families on a tight budget, a fast food meal can seem like a practical and affordable option. Chains such as McDonald's and KFC are known for offering value menus and special deals that make their food accessible to a wide range of customers, including those with very limited incomes. This affordability has played a major role in making fast food part of everyday life for millions of households.", "Taste and familiarity are also powerful draws. Fast food companies spend enormous amounts of money developing recipes that are specifically designed to be enjoyable — often combining salt, sugar, and fat in ways that many people find highly satisfying. Once customers become used to a particular flavor or product, they tend to return to it regularly, creating strong brand loyalty. Children who grow up eating fast food often continue to prefer it as adults, meaning that the habit can last a lifetime. In addition, the global standardization of fast food menus means that a customer traveling to another country can walk into a familiar chain and order exactly the same meal they would have at home, which provides a sense of comfort and reliability.", "Despite its widespread appeal, fast food has attracted considerable criticism. Health experts have repeatedly warned that diets high in fast food are linked to a range of serious medical conditions, including obesity, heart disease, and type 2 diabetes. Many fast food items contain very high levels of calories, saturated fat, and sodium, while offering limited amounts of vitamins, minerals, and dietary fiber. Several governments around the world have responded by introducing measures such as calorie labeling on menus, taxes on sugary drinks, and restrictions on fast food advertising directed at children. However, critics argue that these measures do not go far enough and that more decisive action is needed to protect public health.", "The fast food industry has also faced growing pressure to become more environmentally responsible. The packaging used by fast food outlets — including plastic cups, paper bags, cardboard boxes, and plastic straws — generates enormous quantities of waste each year. Some major chains have begun replacing single-use plastics with biodegradable alternatives and have introduced recycling programs in their restaurants. Others have added plant-based menu items in response to concerns about the environmental impact of meat production. Whether these efforts represent genuine commitment or merely a public relations strategy is a matter of ongoing debate among consumers and environmental campaigners alike."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main idea of the passage?", options: ["Fast food restaurants have become too expensive for low-income families in many countries", "Governments need to introduce stricter laws to limit the number of fast food outlets in cities", "Fast food has grown widely popular due to several practical advantages, though it also raises significant health and environmental concerns", "The fast food industry is primarily responsible for rising obesity rates around the world"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, which feature of fast food restaurants allows customers to get their food without leaving their vehicles?", options: ["Extended opening hours that include early mornings and late evenings", "Drive-through services available at many outlets", "Pre-packaged frozen meals sold in supermarkets", "Online delivery applications that bring food directly to the customer's home"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, why do families with limited budgets often choose fast food?", options: ["Fast food is delivered faster than groceries can be purchased from a supermarket", "Fast food chains offer discounted prices and value menus that make meals affordable for a wide range of customers", "Fast food restaurants provide free meals to customers who cannot afford to pay", "Cooking at home always requires expensive kitchen equipment that many families do not own"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, why do many people find fast food particularly enjoyable to eat?", options: ["Fast food companies hire professional chefs trained in international cuisine", "Fast food menus are constantly updated to reflect the latest global food trends", "Fast food recipes use only natural and organic ingredients sourced from local farms", "The recipes are carefully developed to combine salt, sugar, and fat in proportions that most people find highly satisfying"], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a health condition linked to frequent fast food consumption?", options: ["Obesity", "Type 2 diabetes", "High blood pressure", "Heart disease"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following actions have some governments taken in response to the health risks of fast food?", options: ["Introducing calorie labeling on menus, taxes on sugary drinks, and restrictions on advertising to children", "Requiring fast food companies to donate a portion of their profits to public health programs", "Banning fast food outlets from operating within a certain distance of schools", "Setting a legal maximum on the number of fast food restaurants permitted in each city"], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"decisive\" in paragraph 5 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["firm and having a clear, strong effect", "popular among the general public", "based on scientific research and data", "gradual and carefully planned"], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"they\" in paragraph 4 refers to _______.", options: ["customers who have become accustomed to a particular flavor or product", "fast food companies that invest heavily in recipe development", "health experts who study the effects of diet on the human body", "children who grow up eating fast food regularly"], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"biodegradable\" in paragraph 6 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["designed to be reused many times without losing its original quality", "capable of being broken down naturally by living organisms over time", "tested and approved by environmental authorities for safe public use", "produced using renewable energy sources during the manufacturing process"], answer: 1 }, { q: "What can be inferred about the author's attitude toward the fast food industry's environmental efforts described in paragraph 6?", options: ["The author is fully convinced that the industry's environmental measures are sincere and effective", "The author is uncertain whether the industry's environmental actions reflect genuine commitment or merely serve as a public image strategy", "The author suggests that consumers are unaware of the environmental damage caused by fast food packaging", "The author believes that fast food companies should be legally required to eliminate all plastic packaging immediately"], answer: 1 }] },
    { title: "Navigating a Career Change", paragraphs: ["At some point in their working lives, many people begin to feel that the career they have built no longer suits who they are or what they want from life. Perhaps the work has become repetitive and uninspiring, or a change in personal circumstances has shifted their priorities. Whatever the reason, the decision to change careers is rarely simple. It involves a mix of practical challenges — such as retraining, updating qualifications, and competing with younger candidates — and deeper psychological questions about identity, risk, and self-worth. Understanding how to approach this process thoughtfully can make the difference between a transition that feels overwhelming and one that opens up genuinely new possibilities.", "The first step in any career change is honest self-assessment. Before exploring specific opportunities, individuals benefit greatly from taking time to identify their transferable skills — abilities and knowledge acquired in one field that can be applied usefully in another. A teacher moving into corporate training, for example, brings strong communication, curriculum design, and classroom management skills that are directly relevant to the new role. Similarly, a nurse transitioning into health policy or medical writing carries a depth of clinical knowledge and patient-centered thinking that few candidates from other backgrounds can match. Recognizing the value of what one already knows, rather than focusing solely on gaps, is a psychologically important starting point.", "Financial planning is another dimension that requires early attention. Career transitions frequently involve a period of reduced income — whether because of part-time study, an unpaid internship, or an entry-level position in a new field. Experts recommend building an emergency fund covering at least three to six months of living expenses before beginning a transition, and being realistic about how long the process may take. Some career changes can be completed within a year; others, particularly those requiring new professional licenses or postgraduate qualifications, may take three years or more. Setting a realistic budget and timeline at the outset helps prevent financial stress from derailing what might otherwise be a well-planned transition.", "Networking plays a pivotal role in successful career changes, often proving more decisive than qualifications alone. Many job openings — particularly at mid-career level — are filled through personal connections rather than formal advertising. Attending industry events, joining professional associations, and reaching out to contacts in the target field for informal conversations can provide both practical information and access to opportunities that are never publicly listed. Online platforms such as LinkedIn have made it considerably easier to build connections across industries, and career changers who use these tools strategically often find that a single well-placed introduction can open doors that months of unsolicited applications could not.", "One of the most psychologically demanding aspects of a career change is the temporary loss of status and expertise that it often entails . A senior manager moving into a completely new industry may find herself starting as a junior employee, learning basic procedures that much younger colleagues already know well. This reversal can be deeply uncomfortable, particularly for individuals whose professional identity is closely tied to their position or title. Psychologists who study career transitions note that reframing this period as an investment rather than a setback — recognizing that competence is being built, not lost — is one of the most effective mental strategies available to career changers.", "It is also worth noting that not all career changes require starting from scratch. In many cases, individuals can make a gradual shift by taking on freelance projects, volunteer work, or part-time roles in their target field while still employed in their current job. This approach has the advantage of reducing financial risk while allowing the individual to test whether the new field genuinely suits them before making a full commitment. Career counselors often refer to this strategy as \"bridge employment\" — a way of spanning the gap between one career and the next without the anxiety of an abrupt leap into the unknown.", "Ultimately, a career change is as much a journey of personal development as it is a professional transition. Those who navigate it most successfully tend to share certain qualities: intellectual curiosity, a tolerance for temporary uncertainty, and the willingness to seek guidance from mentors, career coaches, or professional networks. The process is rarely linear, and setbacks are common. But for those who approach it with preparation, flexibility, and realistic expectations, a career change can represent one of the most rewarding decisions of a working life."], questions: [{ q: "What is the passage mainly about?", options: ["The reasons why people become dissatisfied with their current jobs and decide to resign", "A practical and psychological guide to managing the various challenges involved in changing careers", "How online networking platforms have made it easier for professionals to find new employment", "The financial risks associated with leaving a stable job to pursue a completely different profession"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, what does the example of the nurse transitioning into health policy or medical writing illustrate?", options: ["That expertise built in one profession can provide a meaningful advantage when entering a related new field", "That nurses are among the most successful professionals when it comes to changing careers", "That people changing careers should focus on gaps in their knowledge rather than existing strengths", "That healthcare professionals earn significantly higher salaries when they move into policy roles"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, what do experts recommend individuals save before beginning a career transition?", options: ["A fixed percentage of their monthly salary, saved over a period of at least two years", "Only the amount needed to pay for the first semester of a new qualification or training course", "A financial reserve sufficient to cover between three and six months of everyday living costs", "Enough money to cover an entire year of living expenses as well as the cost of any retraining program"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a networking strategy recommended for career changers?", options: ["Having informal conversations with contacts already working in the desired field", "Hiring a professional recruitment agent to distribute a resume to multiple employers", "Attending events organized within the target industry", "Joining associations relevant to the new professional field"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, why do psychologists suggest reframing the early stage of a career change as an \"investment\"?", options: ["Because it motivates individuals to work harder than their younger colleagues in the new workplace", "Because it reminds career changers that entry-level salaries will eventually increase significantly", "Because it helps people cope with the discomfort of temporarily losing seniority by recognizing that competence is being developed", "Because it encourages individuals to spend money on high-quality training programs without hesitation"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, what is one key advantage of the \"bridge employment\" approach?", options: ["It guarantees that individuals will receive a job offer in their new field within six months", "It requires individuals to commit fully to the new career before leaving their current position", "It allows career changers to test a new field and reduce financial risk while still earning income from their current job", "It provides access to government funding for retraining programs not available to those who resign immediately"], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"pivotal\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["time-consuming but ultimately rewarding", "of central and critical importance to the outcome", "related to digital tools and online communication", "optional and largely a matter of personal preference"], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"entails\" in paragraph 5 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["compensates for or makes up for a previous loss", "describes or explains in clear and simple terms", "involves or necessarily brings with it as a consequence", "prevents or blocks from happening"], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"these\" in paragraph 4 refers to _______.", options: ["informal conversations held with contacts already working in the target field", "industry events and professional association meetings attended by career changers", "online platforms such as LinkedIn used to build cross-industry connections", "mid-career job openings that are filled through personal connections rather than advertising"], answer: 2 }, { q: "Based on the passage, what can be inferred about people who successfully change careers?", options: ["They usually have access to a large professional network built over many years in their original field", "They are typically younger professionals who have fewer financial obligations and more flexibility", "They almost always choose fields closely related to their original profession to minimize the difficulty of the transition", "Their success depends more on mindset, preparation, and adaptability than on any single practical factor"], answer: 3 }] },
    { title: "The Global Transition to Renewable Energy", paragraphs: ["The shift away from fossil fuels toward renewable sources of energy represents one of the most significant economic and technological transformations of the twenty-first century. Driven by mounting scientific evidence of climate change, falling costs of clean energy technologies, and increasing pressure from both governments and investors, the global energy system is undergoing a structural reorganization that few analysts predicted would proceed at its current pace. Yet despite encouraging headlines about record solar installations and rapidly expanding wind capacity, the full transition remains a complex and deeply uneven process — one shaped as much by politics and infrastructure as by engineering innovation.", "Solar and wind power have emerged as the twin engines of the clean energy revolution. The cost of utility-scale solar photovoltaic electricity has fallen by more than 89 percent over the past decade, making it the cheapest source of new electricity generation in the majority of the world's markets. Wind energy has followed a similar trajectory, with offshore wind projects now routinely achieving costs that would have seemed implausible a generation ago. These cost reductions have been driven by economies of scale in manufacturing, advances in materials science, and the accumulated experience of installing thousands of projects across diverse geographic conditions. As a result, investment in new renewable capacity now consistently outpaces investment in new fossil fuel infrastructure at the global level.", "However, the rapid growth of intermittent renewables introduces a fundamental challenge for electricity grid operators: the problem of variability. Solar panels generate power only when the sun shines; wind turbines produce electricity only when the wind blows. [1] This mismatch between supply and demand requires grid operators to develop new tools for balancing the system, including large-scale battery storage, interconnected transmission networks that allow power to flow between regions, and demand-response programs that incentivize consumers to shift their electricity use to periods of high renewable output. [2] Pumped hydro storage — in which water is pumped uphill during periods of excess supply and released through turbines when demand rises — currently accounts for the majority of the world's grid-scale energy storage, though battery technologies are advancing rapidly. [3] Each of these solutions carries its own costs, technical constraints, and political complications, and no single approach is sufficient on its own. [4]", "The transition is also deeply shaped by geography and economic development. Countries such as Denmark, Germany, and Portugal have made rapid progress in deploying renewables, in part because they have well-developed grid infrastructure, relatively small territories that simplify transmission planning, and strong political consensus in favor of climate action. By contrast, many developing nations in sub-Saharan Africa and South Asia face a different set of priorities: hundreds of millions of people still lack reliable access to electricity of any kind, and the immediate imperative of expanding energy access often takes precedence over the longer-term goal of decarbonizing an existing grid. For these countries, the most promising pathway may not be replacing centralized coal plants with centralized renewables, but rather deploying small-scale solar and battery systems directly at the household or community level — a model that bypasses the need for expensive grid infrastructure entirely.", "The fossil fuel industry, meanwhile, has not been a passive observer of these changes. Major oil and gas companies have responded in divergent ways: some have invested substantially in renewable energy divisions, repositioning themselves as broad energy companies; others have doubled down on hydrocarbon extraction, betting that global energy demand will remain high enough to justify continued investment in oil and gas fields. National oil companies in producer states such as Saudi Arabia, Russia, and the UAE face a particular strategic dilemma, as their national budgets are deeply dependent on oil revenues that could be threatened by an accelerating energy transition. The political economy of these transitions is therefore as important as the technological dimension.", "Public policy has played an indispensable role in driving the clean energy transition forward. Feed-in tariffs, renewable portfolio standards, carbon pricing mechanisms, and direct government subsidies have all been deployed — with varying degrees of success — to stimulate investment in clean energy and penalize continued reliance on fossil fuels. The United States Inflation Reduction Act of 2022, which allocated nearly 370 billion dollars toward clean energy investment over a decade, is widely regarded as the most significant climate legislation in American history and has triggered a wave of manufacturing investment in solar panels, batteries, and electric vehicles. Similar policy frameworks are being developed or strengthened across the European Union, China, and India, reflecting a growing recognition that market forces alone are insufficient to drive the transition at the pace required.", "Whether the global energy transition will proceed quickly enough to limit warming to the targets set by the Paris Agreement remains deeply uncertain. The International Energy Agency has noted that while the growth of renewables is accelerating, global carbon emissions from the energy sector have not yet begun the sustained decline that climate science says is necessary. Closing this gap will require not only continued technological progress and investment, but also a level of international cooperation, political will, and institutional capacity that has so far proved difficult to sustain. The transition to renewable energy is underway — but its ultimate scale and speed will be determined by choices that societies have yet to make."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main idea of the passage?", options: ["Developing nations are the main obstacle preventing the world from achieving a complete clean energy transition", "Government subsidies are the single most important driver of renewable energy growth worldwide", "The global shift to renewable energy is progressing rapidly but remains complicated by technological, geographic, and political factors", "Solar and wind power have already replaced fossil fuels as the primary source of electricity in most countries"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, what has caused the dramatic reduction in the cost of solar photovoltaic electricity over the past decade?", options: ["International agreements that required solar manufacturers to lower their prices for developing countries", "Direct government subsidies that have offset the true cost of solar panel production", "A combination of manufacturing economies of scale, materials science advances, and accumulated installation experience", "The discovery of new raw materials that made solar panels significantly cheaper to produce"], answer: 2 }, { q: "Which of the following square brackets [1] , [2] , [3] , or [4] best indicates where the following sentence could be added? 'Neither source can be relied upon to deliver power at precisely the time when consumers need it most.'", options: ["[1]", "[2]", "[3]", "[4]"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following best explains why Denmark, Germany, and Portugal have advanced more quickly in deploying renewable energy than many developing nations?", options: ["They have access to superior wind and solar resources that make renewable generation more efficient", "They benefit from established grid infrastructure, compact territories, and broad political support for climate action", "They receive substantial financial support from international climate funds that developing nations cannot access", "Their populations are smaller and therefore require less total electricity, making the transition easier to manage"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, what approach does the passage suggest may be most suitable for developing nations that still lack reliable electricity access?", options: ["Deploying decentralized solar and battery systems at the household or community level to avoid costly grid construction", "Importing surplus renewable electricity from neighboring countries through cross-border transmission lines", "Building large centralized renewable power plants to replace existing coal infrastructure", "Accepting continued reliance on fossil fuels until national income levels rise sufficiently"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, what has been a notable consequence of the United States Inflation Reduction Act of 2022?", options: ["It has required all American power companies to switch entirely to renewable sources by 2030", "It has provided direct cash payments to American households that install rooftop solar panels", "It has prompted a significant wave of manufacturing investment in solar panels, batteries, and electric vehicles", "It has funded the construction of the world's largest offshore wind farm off the coast of California"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a solution to the variability problem created by intermittent renewable energy sources?", options: ["Demand-response programs that shift consumer electricity use", "Large-scale battery storage systems", "Pumped hydro storage that uses surplus energy to move water uphill", "Nuclear power plants that provide constant baseline electricity output"], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"implausible\" in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["extremely competitive in global markets", "difficult to believe or accept as realistic", "dependent on government financial support", "closely linked to advances in digital technology"], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"bypasses\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["reduces the overall cost of", "improves the efficiency of", "replaces the function of", "avoids the need for entirely"], answer: 3 }, { q: "What can be inferred from the final paragraph about the current state of the global energy transition?", options: ["The International Energy Agency believes that market forces alone will be sufficient to complete the energy transition", "Renewable energy growth is accelerating, but carbon emissions have not yet declined in a way consistent with international climate targets", "The transition is essentially complete in developed nations and needs only to be extended to poorer countries", "International cooperation on climate change has already reached the level required to limit warming to Paris Agreement goals"], answer: 1 }] },
    { title: "Tracing the Milestones of Human Evolution", paragraphs: ["The story of human evolution is not a neat, linear progression from primitive ancestor to modern Homo sapiens, but rather a richly branching tree of species, adaptations, and extinctions that unfolded over approximately six million years. [1] Each new fossil discovery or genomic analysis has the potential to revise established timelines, reposition species within the evolutionary tree, or reveal patterns of interbreeding that were previously unsuspected. [2] What paleoanthropologists have constructed is therefore best understood not as a fixed account, but as an evolving framework — one that accommodates new evidence with each passing decade. [3] The broad outlines of the story, however, have become considerably clearer since the molecular revolution of the late twentieth century transformed the field. [4]", "The earliest members of the human lineage — the hominins — are thought to have diverged from the last common ancestor shared with chimpanzees somewhere between six and seven million years ago in Africa. Among the earliest candidates for this distinction is Sahelanthropus tchadensis, a species known from a single, heavily weathered skull discovered in Chad in 2001. The skull's combination of a small brain case and a foramen magnum — the opening at the base of the skull through which the spinal cord passes — positioned in a manner more consistent with upright walking than with knuckle-walking, has led some researchers to argue that it represents one of the oldest known bipeds. Others remain skeptical, pointing to the fragmentary nature of the fossil record and the absence of post-cranial bones that would confirm habitual bipedalism.", "Bipedalism — the ability to walk upright on two legs — is widely regarded as one of the most consequential adaptations in the history of the human lineage. The advantages it conferred are debated, but leading hypotheses include the freeing of the hands for tool use and food carrying, improved energy efficiency over long distances compared to quadrupedal locomotion, and the ability to scan the horizon for predators or resources across open savannah environments. The emergence of bipedalism substantially preceded the expansion of brain size that would later come to define the genus Homo, suggesting that walking upright was not a consequence of growing intelligence but rather a precondition that set the stage for subsequent cognitive development.", "The australopithecines — a diverse group of bipedal hominins that flourished across Africa between roughly four and two million years ago — represent the next major chapter in the story. The most famous individual from this group is AL 288-1, better known as \"Lucy,\" a female Australopithecus afarensis whose remarkably complete skeleton was unearthed in Ethiopia in 1974. Lucy stood approximately 1.1 meters tall, walked upright, and had a brain roughly one-third the size of a modern human's. Her discovery consolidated the view, previously contested, that bipedalism preceded significant brain enlargement in hominin evolution. More recent discoveries, including a juvenile Australopithecus afarensis skeleton known as \"Selam\" found in 2000, have added further nuance by revealing that the shoulder anatomy of early australopithecines retained features associated with arboreal locomotion, suggesting that these hominins were capable of both walking on the ground and climbing in trees.", "The emergence of the genus Homo approximately 2.5 to 3 million years ago marked a significant shift. Early Homo species — including Homo habilis and Homo erectus — were associated with a substantial increase in brain size, the systematic production of stone tools, and, in the case of Homo erectus, the first major dispersal of hominins out of Africa and into Eurasia. Homo erectus survived for an extraordinarily long period — possibly nearly two million years — and its fossils have been recovered from sites as geographically distant as Georgia, China, and the Indonesian island of Java. The longevity and geographic spread of this species underscore the remarkable adaptability that had come to characterize the human lineage.", "The relationship between archaic human populations and anatomically modern Homo sapiens, who emerged in Africa approximately 300,000 years ago, has been dramatically redrawn by ancient DNA research. Analysis of ancient genomes has revealed that modern humans interbred with at least two archaic populations after leaving Africa: the Neanderthals, who inhabited Europe and western Asia, and the Denisovans, a group known primarily from fragmentary remains found in a Siberian cave. Most people of non-African descent carry roughly one to two percent Neanderthal DNA, while elevated levels of Denisovan ancestry are detectable in populations from Oceania and parts of Southeast Asia. These findings demonstrate that the emergence of modern humans was not a clean replacement of earlier populations but a complex process involving admixture, competition, and coexistence.", "What drove the eventual extinction of archaic human species remains a matter of active debate. Climate fluctuations, competition for resources with expanding Homo sapiens populations, and susceptibility to pathogens carried by modern humans have all been proposed as contributing factors. The archaeological record suggests that the disappearance of Neanderthals in Europe roughly 40,000 years ago coincided with the arrival of anatomically modern humans and a suite of behavioral innovations — more sophisticated tool technologies, symbolic art, and expanded long-distance exchange networks — that may have conferred decisive advantages. Whether this transition involved violent displacement, ecological marginalization, or gradual population absorption through interbreeding is a question that the available evidence does not yet resolve.", "The study of human evolution continues to be transformed by methodological innovation. Ancient DNA extraction, isotopic analysis of fossil teeth, high-resolution computed tomography scanning of skull interiors, and the growing application of machine learning to the classification of fragmentary fossils are all expanding the range of questions that can now be addressed. Each of these tools brings its own interpretive challenges, and the field remains characterized by vigorous disagreement among specialists. What is not in doubt, however, is that the picture of human origins emerging from this research is far more complex, far more dynamic, and far more surprising than the linear narratives that once dominated textbooks."], questions: [{ q: "Which of the following square brackets [1] , [2] , [3] , or [4] best indicates where the following sentence could be added? 'This complexity means that even well-established conclusions must remain open to revision as new data emerge.'", options: ["[1]", "[2]", "[3]", "[4]"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, why do some researchers regard Sahelanthropus tchadensis as significant, while others remain skeptical?", options: ["Supporters argue its brain size indicates early language ability, while skeptics note its teeth resemble those of non-hominin primates", "Supporters claim the skull was found alongside stone tools, while skeptics argue the tools belonged to a different species", "Supporters believe its geographic location in Chad suggests early migration out of Africa, while skeptics dispute the dating of the fossil", "Supporters cite the position of its foramen magnum as evidence of bipedalism, while skeptics note the lack of post-cranial bones to confirm this"], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a proposed advantage of bipedalism?", options: ["Freeing the hands for carrying food and making tools", "An improved capacity to survey the surrounding environment in open landscapes", "Greater energy efficiency when traveling over long distances", "The ability to regulate body temperature more effectively in hot climates"], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, what was the significance of the discovery of Lucy (AL 288-1)?", options: ["It reinforced the previously debated conclusion that upright walking evolved before significant increases in brain size", "It demonstrated that australopithecines were able to run at speeds comparable to modern long-distance runners", "It proved that Australopithecus afarensis was the direct ancestor of all later members of the genus Homo", "It provided the first evidence that early hominins were capable of producing and using stone tools"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, what does the geographic range of Homo erectus fossil sites suggest about this species?", options: ["That Homo erectus possessed a remarkable capacity to survive and thrive across vastly different environments", "That Homo erectus was more closely related to modern humans than previously believed based on genetic evidence", "That Homo erectus traveled between continents using primitive watercraft", "That Homo erectus had a highly developed social structure that enabled coordinated long-distance migration"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, what does ancient DNA research reveal about the emergence of anatomically modern Homo sapiens?", options: ["Modern humans interbred with at least two archaic populations after leaving Africa, leaving detectable genetic traces in living people today", "All modern human populations carry equal proportions of both Neanderthal and Denisovan ancestry", "The Denisovans were more closely related to modern humans than the Neanderthals were", "Modern humans completely replaced all archaic populations without any genetic exchange taking place"], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"consolidated\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["challenged and ultimately disproved through new fossil evidence", "introduced for the first time into scientific discussion", "simplified so as to be accessible to a general audience", "strengthened and made more firmly established"], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"adaptability\" in paragraph 5 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["the capacity to adjust successfully to a wide range of different conditions", "the ability to develop complex social hierarchies within large groups", "the drive to compete aggressively with rival species for shared resources", "the tendency to pass favorable genetic traits to successive generations"], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"it\" in paragraph 2 refers to _______.", options: ["the fragmentary nature of the fossil record in Chad", "the single weathered skull discovered in 2001", "Sahelanthropus tchadensis as a species", "the foramen magnum found at the base of the skull"], answer: 2 }, { q: "Based on paragraphs 1 and 8, which of the following best describes the author's view of the field of paleoanthropology?", options: ["It is a mature science whose core conclusions are now beyond meaningful dispute", "It is hindered primarily by a lack of funding, which prevents researchers from collecting the fossil evidence needed to resolve key debates", "It is a dynamic and self-correcting field whose understanding of human origins grows more complex and surprising with each new discovery", "It has been overly dominated by DNA research at the expense of traditional fossil analysis"], answer: 2 }] },
  ] },
  { id: "rd-066", num: 66, title: "Đề 066", timeMin: 60, parts: [
    { title: "The Benefits of Community Sports", paragraphs: ["Community sports have long been a part of everyday life in towns and cities around the world. Whether it is a weekly football match in the local park, a swimming club that meets every Saturday morning, or a neighborhood basketball team, community sports bring people of all ages together in a fun and healthy way. More and more people are discovering that joining a community sports team is not just about playing a game — it is about becoming part of something bigger than themselves.", "One of the most obvious benefits of community sports is the improvement in physical health. Playing sports regularly helps the body stay strong and fit. People who take part in physical activities at least three times a week are less likely to develop serious health problems such as heart disease, obesity, and type 2 diabetes. Even gentle activities like walking or playing a slow-paced game of tennis can have a positive effect on the body. Doctors often advocate regular physical activity as one of the simplest ways to lead a longer, healthier life.", "Community sports also have a powerful effect on mental health. Many people feel stressed after a long day at work or school. Joining a sports team gives them a chance to take a break from daily worries and focus on something enjoyable. Studies have shown that physical activity causes the brain to release chemicals called endorphins, which are natural mood-lifters. People who exercise regularly often report feeling happier and more relaxed than those who do not. For many players, sports practice several times a week also leads to better sleep at night.", "Another important benefit is the sense of community and belonging that sports can create. When people play together regularly, they build friendships and learn to trust one another. These bonds can be especially valuable for people who are new to a neighborhood or who feel lonely. Local sports clubs also give communities a sense of shared identity. Supporting the same team, wearing the same colors, and celebrating victories together can make a group of strangers feel like a family. In many towns, the local sports club is one of the most important social centers in the area.", "Children and young people benefit greatly from taking part in community sports. Learning to play as part of a team teaches important life skills such as cooperation, patience, and respect for others. Young players must learn to follow rules, accept a loss gracefully, and encourage their teammates. These lessons stay with children long after they leave the sports field and help them become more responsible and confident adults. Parents often notice that children who are involved in regular sports activities perform better at school and are less likely to get into trouble.", "Community sports also give people a chance to develop leadership skills. Team captains, coaches, and club organizers all take on roles that require responsibility, communication, and problem-solving. Even ordinary players can develop a greater sense of discipline and goal-setting by training regularly and working to improve their performance. Many adults credit their time playing community sports as one of the most valuable learning experiences of their lives.", "Despite all these benefits, some people feel that community sports are not for them. They may worry about not being good enough to join a team, or they may feel that they simply do not have enough time. However, most community sports clubs welcome players of all ability levels and offer flexible training schedules to suit busy lifestyles. The important thing is not to win every match, but to enjoy being active and connected with others. In short, community sports offer something valuable for everyone, regardless of age, background, or experience level."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main idea of the passage?", options: ["Community sports are only suitable for people who already have good fitness levels.", "Children benefit more from community sports than adults do.", "Joining a community sports team offers a wide range of physical, mental, and social advantages.", "Community sports are primarily popular because they help people win competitions."], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, how often should people take part in physical activities to reduce the risk of serious health problems?", options: ["At least once a week", "No fewer than three times per week", "Two times a month at minimum", "Every day without exception"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, what happens in the brain when a person exercises?", options: ["The brain stores more memories related to physical movement.", "The brain produces more sleep hormones, causing tiredness.", "The brain slows down its activity to help the body recover.", "The brain releases natural chemicals that improve a person's mood."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is a life skill that young players can develop through community sports?", options: ["Knowing how to accept defeat with dignity and support teammates", "Learning to prioritize winning over team relationships", "Advanced tactical thinking for professional competitions", "The ability to train independently without a coach"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, who are the people that can develop leadership skills through community sports?", options: ["Only professional athletes who train full time", "Team captains, coaches, organizers, and even regular players", "Young children who start playing sports before the age of ten", "Adults who have previously worked in management roles"], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"them\" in paragraph 3 refers to _______.", options: ["coaches who run training sessions", "people who feel stressed after a long day", "endorphins released during exercise", "sports teams in the community"], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"advocate\" in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["demand officially", "criticize", "observe carefully", "recommend strongly"], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"bonds\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["close connections formed between people", "physical ties used in sport equipment", "official contracts signed by club members", "financial agreements between players"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a benefit of playing community sports for children?", options: ["Learning to cooperate with teammates", "Earning scholarships to professional sports academies", "Becoming more patient and respectful", "Performing better at school"], answer: 1 }, { q: "What can be inferred about the author's attitude toward people who think community sports are not for them?", options: ["The author believes their concerns are completely valid and understands why they avoid sports.", "The author implies that only highly motivated people will ever join a community sports club.", "The author thinks they are making excuses and should be criticized for being inactive.", "The author suggests that their worries can be addressed since clubs are welcoming and flexible."], answer: 3 }] },
    { title: "The Majesty of the Blue Whale", paragraphs: ["The blue whale (Balaenoptera musculus) holds the distinction of being the largest animal ever known to have existed on Earth. These magnificent marine mammals can reach lengths of up to 30 meters — roughly the size of three double-decker buses placed end to end — and can weigh as much as 200 tonnes. Their hearts alone are approximately the size of a small car, and the sound of their heartbeat can be detected from several kilometers away. Despite their overwhelming size, blue whales move through the ocean with a surprising grace and efficiency that has fascinated scientists, sailors, and naturalists for centuries.", "Blue whales are found in every ocean on the planet, though their distribution is far from uniform throughout the year. They tend to spend the warmer summer months feeding in the cold, nutrient-rich waters near the polar regions, where vast quantities of their primary food source — tiny shrimp-like creatures called krill — are available in dense concentrations. As winter approaches and the polar waters begin to freeze, blue whales migrate toward warmer tropical and subtropical waters, where they breed and give birth. This annual journey can cover tens of thousands of kilometers, making it one of the longest migrations undertaken by any animal.", "The feeding habits of the blue whale are both remarkable and highly specialized. Unlike many predators that actively pursue individual prey, the blue whale employs a technique known as lunge feeding. The animal accelerates toward a dense swarm of krill, opens its enormous mouth, and takes in a colossal volume of water along with the krill. It then uses its baleen plates — rows of flexible, comb-like structures made of keratin that hang from the upper jaw — to filter the krill out of the water before swallowing. A single adult blue whale may consume up to four tonnes of krill in a single day during the peak feeding season, accumulating vast energy reserves to sustain it throughout the long migration and breeding period when food is scarce.", "Despite being the largest creature on Earth, the blue whale produces no sound that humans can hear without specialized equipment. The calls of the blue whale are emitted at extremely low frequencies — so low that they fall below the range of human hearing — yet these infrasonic vocalizations can travel hundreds or even thousands of kilometers through the ocean. Scientists believe that blue whales use these calls primarily for long-distance communication, particularly during the mating season, when individual whales may be separated by vast expanses of open ocean. The precise meanings of the different call patterns remain an active area of scientific research.", "Blue whales were once abundant across all the world's oceans, but their populations were severely reduced during the era of commercial whaling in the nineteenth and twentieth centuries. At the height of the whaling industry, hunters targeted blue whales with particular intensity because of their enormous size, which yielded exceptionally large quantities of oil and other commercially valuable products. By the middle of the twentieth century, it is estimated that global blue whale populations had declined by as much as 97 percent from their pre-whaling levels. The International Whaling Commission introduced a global ban on commercial blue whale hunting in 1966, and since then, populations have shown slow but measurable signs of recovery in some regions.", "However, the recovery of the blue whale remains fragile and is threatened by a range of ongoing pressures. Ship strikes represent one of the most immediate dangers, as blue whales often rest near the ocean surface and are difficult for vessel crews to detect in time to avoid a collision. Noise pollution from shipping traffic, sonar systems, and industrial activities is also a serious concern, as it may interfere with the whales' ability to communicate and navigate effectively. Additionally, climate change poses a long-term threat by altering ocean temperatures and disrupting the distribution and abundance of krill, which could undermine the whales' ability to accumulate sufficient energy reserves during the feeding season.", "Conservation organizations and governments around the world have implemented a range of measures aimed at protecting blue whale populations. These include establishing marine protected areas in key feeding and breeding grounds, developing vessel speed restrictions and routing measures in areas with high whale activity, and investing in acoustic monitoring programs to track population movements and study communication patterns. While the blue whale remains classified as an endangered species, the combination of legal protection and dedicated conservation efforts offers a cautious basis for optimism about the long-term survival of the largest animal on Earth."], questions: [{ q: "What is the passage mainly about?", options: ["An overview of the blue whale's biology, behavior, threats, and conservation", "How climate change is causing the extinction of endangered ocean animals", "A scientific comparison between different species of marine mammals", "The history of the whaling industry and its economic importance"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, how much krill can an adult blue whale consume in a single day during peak feeding season?", options: ["Between two and three tonnes", "As much as four tonnes", "More than ten tonnes", "Up to one tonne"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, when did the International Whaling Commission prohibit commercial hunting of blue whales?", options: ["When blue whale numbers dropped by fifty percent", "At the beginning of the twentieth century", "In 1966", "After the Second World War ended in 1945"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, what is the technique called when a blue whale rushes toward a group of krill and takes in a large amount of water through its open mouth?", options: ["Lunge feeding", "Filter sweeping", "Krill scooping", "Baleen trapping"], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"migrate\" in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["return permanently to a warmer home territory", "dive to great ocean depths to avoid cold temperatures", "travel seasonally from one region to another", "search for a new food source in unfamiliar waters"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following correctly describes a current threat to blue whales?", options: ["Competition for krill with other large whale species", "Hunting by local fishing communities in tropical waters", "Collisions with vessels whose crews cannot detect the whales in time", "Predation by great white sharks in polar feeding grounds"], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"accumulating\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["distributing evenly", "converting immediately", "building up gradually", "spending rapidly"], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"these\" in paragraph 4 refers to _______.", options: ["the long distances across the open ocean", "the specialized equipment used by scientists", "the very low-frequency calls produced by blue whales", "blue whales in general"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a current threat to blue whale populations?", options: ["Disruption caused by underwater noise pollution", "Illegal hunting by organized poaching networks", "Collisions with large ships", "Changes in krill availability caused by shifting ocean temperatures"], answer: 1 }, { q: "What is the author's attitude toward the future survival of the blue whale?", options: ["Carefully hopeful, acknowledging that recovery is possible but still uncertain", "Deeply pessimistic, believing the species is certain to become extinct", "Neutral and detached, presenting only scientific data without any evaluative judgment", "Entirely optimistic, arguing that conservation efforts have already solved the problem"], answer: 0 }] },
    { title: "Podcasts as a Tool for Mental Healing", paragraphs: ["Over the past decade, podcasting has evolved from a niche digital hobby into one of the most widely consumed forms of media on the planet. With more than five million active shows and hundreds of millions of listeners worldwide, the podcast has established itself as a versatile and intimate medium uniquely suited to the rhythms of modern life. People listen while commuting, exercising, cooking, or lying awake at night when sleep refuses to come. It is in precisely these unguarded, solitary moments that a particular category of podcast — those focused on mental health, emotional wellbeing, and psychological healing — has begun to demonstrate an influence that researchers and clinicians are only beginning to map with any systematic rigor.", "The therapeutic potential of listening is not a new idea. Long before the podcast existed, the practice of storytelling, communal sharing, and the spoken word formed the backbone of healing traditions in cultures across the world. What podcasting has done is to scale and democratize these traditions in ways that earlier formats could not. A listener in a rural village in Indonesia can now access the same conversation between a clinical psychologist and a trauma survivor that a resident of New York or London might download during a morning run. This accessibility is more than a logistical convenience — for many people, it represents the first meaningful contact they have had with the language, frameworks, and lived experiences that mental health discourse makes available. In communities where mental illness carries significant stigma, the private, earphone-mediated nature of podcast listening allows individuals to engage with sensitive material without the social exposure that seeking in-person help can involve. [1]", "Researchers studying the psychological effects of mental health podcasts have identified several mechanisms through which listening may confer therapeutic benefit. The first and most consistently reported is the reduction of isolation through what psychologists call \"parasocial relationships\" — the one-sided but emotionally real sense of connection that listeners develop with hosts and guests whose voices become familiar presences in their daily lives. Studies examining parasocial relationships in broadcast media have long established that these connections can provide genuine comfort, reduce loneliness, and in some cases serve as a bridge toward seeking formal therapeutic support. Mental health podcasts, whose content explicitly addresses emotional vulnerability and psychological struggle, appear particularly well-suited to activating this dynamic. [2]", "A second mechanism involves the process of narrative identification — the experience of recognizing one's own emotional circumstances in the stories told by others. When a podcast guest describes living with anxiety, grief, or a sense of purposelessness in terms that a listener finds strikingly resonant with their own inner life, the effect can be one of profound normalization. The implicit message — that these experiences are neither shameful nor unique — counters the cognitive distortions that commonly accompany untreated mental health difficulties, in which sufferers characteristically overestimate how unusual their struggles are and underestimate how widely they are shared. Clinical psychologists have noted that this normalization effect, while insufficient as a standalone treatment, may meaningfully reduce the barriers to help-seeking by diminishing the shame that prevents many individuals from acknowledging their difficulties even to themselves. [3]", "The educational dimension of mental health podcasting deserves equal attention. Many of the most widely listened-to shows in this genre function not merely as emotional companions but as accessible primers in psychological literacy — introducing listeners to concepts from cognitive behavioral therapy, attachment theory, mindfulness, and trauma-informed practice that were previously accessible only to those who could afford professional consultation or had the academic background to navigate clinical literature. This democratization of psychological knowledge carries genuine therapeutic value: listeners who understand the mechanisms underlying their own anxiety or depression are better positioned to engage productively with treatment, communicate their experiences to healthcare providers, and make informed decisions about the kinds of support they need.", "Alongside these genuine benefits, researchers and mental health professionals have raised a number of important cautions. The podcasting space is largely unregulated, and the quality, accuracy, and ethical standards of mental health content vary enormously across shows. Some programs are produced by credentialed clinicians adhering to evidence-based frameworks; others are hosted by individuals with no formal training who may inadvertently disseminate harmful misinformation or frame professional help as unnecessary. A particular concern relates to the risk of what some researchers have termed \"podcast dependency\" — a pattern in which regular listening becomes a substitute for, rather than a supplement to, professional care, leading individuals to delay or forgo treatment in favor of a medium that, however valuable, cannot provide clinical assessment, personalized intervention, or crisis support. [4]", "The most defensible position on the therapeutic value of mental health podcasting is neither uncritical enthusiasm nor reflexive skepticism, but a nuanced recognition that the medium occupies a distinct and genuinely useful position in the broader ecosystem of mental health support — one that is most beneficial when it functions as a point of entry into care rather than a terminus. As access to formal mental healthcare remains severely constrained in most parts of the world by cost, geography, stigma, and workforce shortages, the podcast's capacity to reach individuals who might otherwise receive no support at all represents a contribution that should be taken seriously by clinicians, policymakers, and researchers alike. The challenge is not to romanticize the medium but to understand its actual mechanisms, its real limitations, and the conditions under which it does and does not serve the interests of those who listen."], questions: [{ q: "What is the central concern of the passage?", options: ["The passage argues that podcasts are a superior alternative to traditional therapy for people living in remote areas.", "The passage warns that the lack of regulation in the podcasting industry poses an unacceptable risk to vulnerable listeners.", "The passage examines both the therapeutic potential and the significant limitations of mental health podcasts, concluding that they are most valuable as a gateway to formal care.", "The passage focuses primarily on how the podcasting industry has grown and diversified over the past decade."], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, what are \"parasocial relationships\" as described in the passage?", options: ["One-sided but emotionally genuine connections that listeners develop with podcast hosts and guests over time.", "Therapeutic relationships formed between a patient and a licensed counselor who communicates exclusively through audio recordings.", "Online communities that form around popular mental health podcasts and provide peer support to their members.", "Structured listening programs in which clinicians assign specific podcast episodes to patients as part of a treatment plan."], answer: 0 }, { q: "Based on information from both paragraphs 2 and 6, what is the most accurate assessment of the role mental health podcasts can play?", options: ["They are primarily useful for individuals who have already completed a course of professional therapy and wish to maintain their progress independently.", "They serve a genuine function in reaching underserved populations, but the risk of replacing rather than supplementing professional care means their role must be carefully understood.", "They are most effective as a full replacement for professional therapy in communities where clinical services are unaffordable.", "Their therapeutic value is largely offset by the harm caused by inaccurate content, making broader regulation the most urgent priority."], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"it\" in paragraph 2 refers to _______.", options: ["the ability to access mental health podcast content from anywhere in the world", "the morning run during which many urban listeners typically consume podcasts", "the logistical convenience of downloading podcast episodes", "the clinical psychologist's conversation with a trauma survivor"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, how does the process of narrative identification contribute to mental health benefits for listeners?", options: ["It provides a structured framework through which listeners can evaluate the severity of their own mental health difficulties.", "It teaches listeners specific therapeutic techniques they can apply independently to manage their symptoms.", "It normalizes listeners' emotional struggles by helping them recognize that their experiences are shared by others, potentially reducing shame and barriers to seeking help.", "It allows listeners to rehearse difficult conversations with friends or family members by hearing others model those discussions."], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, what is the concern researchers associate with \"podcast dependency\"?", options: ["The parasocial bonds formed through regular listening may be mistaken for genuine social relationships, reducing motivation to maintain real-world connections.", "Listeners become so emotionally attached to specific hosts that they experience distress when a show ends or a host stops producing content.", "Regular listening becomes a substitute for professional care rather than a supplement to it, causing individuals to delay or avoid treatment.", "Listeners who consume large amounts of mental health content online develop unrealistic expectations of what professional therapy can achieve."], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a psychological concept that mental health podcasts introduce to listeners?", options: ["Cognitive behavioral therapy", "Trauma-informed practice", "Attachment theory", "Dialectical behavior therapy"], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"these\" in paragraph 3 refers to _______.", options: ["clinical psychologists who study the therapeutic effects of media consumption", "parasocial relationships formed between listeners and podcast hosts or guests", "mental health podcasts that address emotional vulnerability and psychological struggle", "studies examining parasocial relationships in broadcast media"], answer: 1 }, { q: "What can be inferred by combining information from paragraphs 4 and 6 about why normalization through podcasts is therapeutically valuable but insufficient on its own?", options: ["Normalization works best for mild anxiety or stress but is counterproductive for individuals with serious mental health conditions who may misidentify their symptoms.", "Normalization helps listeners understand their struggles intellectually, but podcast content is too generalized to address the specific clinical needs of individual listeners.", "Normalization is only effective when delivered by credentialed clinicians, and most podcast hosts do not have the professional training required to facilitate this process.", "Normalization reduces shame and may lower barriers to seeking help, but podcasts cannot provide clinical assessment, personalized intervention, or crisis support when needed."], answer: 3 }, { q: "Which of the following square brackets [1] , [2] , [3] , or [4] best indicates where the following sentence could be added to the passage? \"For individuals in regions where the nearest mental health professional is hours away or where the cost of a single therapy session exceeds a week's earnings, this private mode of engagement with psychological content may be the only accessible starting point.\"", options: ["[1]", "[2]", "[3]", "[4]"], answer: 0 }] },
    { title: "Harnessing the Power of Thermoelectric Energy", paragraphs: ["Among the many frontiers in the global search for cleaner and more efficient energy technologies, thermoelectric generation occupies a position that is simultaneously promising and persistently underexplored. The principle underlying thermoelectric devices is deceptively simple: when a temperature difference exists across a suitable material, that gradient can be converted directly into an electrical current without the need for moving parts, combustion, or any intermediate mechanical process. [1] This phenomenon, described formally by the Seebeck effect — named after the German physicist Thomas Johann Seebeck, who observed it in 1821 — has been understood for nearly two centuries, yet its practical exploitation at meaningful scale has remained elusive, constrained by fundamental material limitations that only the most recent advances in nanotechnology and materials science are beginning to genuinely address .", "The theoretical appeal of thermoelectric generation is substantial. In a world where an estimated sixty to seventy percent of all primary energy consumed by industrial processes is ultimately lost as waste heat — dissipated through exhaust pipes, cooling towers, and the surfaces of industrial machinery — the prospect of recovering even a modest fraction of this thermal loss as usable electricity represents an enormous economic and environmental prize. The automotive sector alone discards sufficient thermal energy through exhaust emissions to power significant electrical loads if that energy could be captured efficiently. Industrial furnaces, data center cooling systems, and geothermal vents represent further reservoirs of untapped thermal potential. The question is not whether the waste heat exists — it does, in prodigious quantities — but whether thermoelectric materials can convert it with sufficient efficiency to justify the cost and complexity of deployment at scale. [2]", "The central technical challenge facing thermoelectric technology is the requirement that an effective thermoelectric material must simultaneously satisfy three largely conflicting material properties. It must possess high electrical conductivity, to allow charge carriers to flow freely and generate useful current. It must exhibit a high Seebeck coefficient, meaning that a given temperature difference must produce a large voltage differential across the material. And it must have low thermal conductivity, so that the temperature gradient driving the electrical conversion is maintained rather than rapidly equalized by heat flowing through the material itself. The difficulty is that these three properties tend to be inversely related in most conventional materials: metals, for instance, are excellent electrical conductors but also excellent thermal conductors, making them poor thermoelectric performers. Semiconductors offer a more favorable balance but have historically achieved only modest efficiencies in practical devices.", "The figure of merit used to evaluate the overall thermoelectric performance of a material is denoted ZT, where higher values correspond to greater efficiency. For decades, ZT values for the best available materials hovered around unity — a threshold widely regarded as insufficient for most commercial applications. The transformation of the field has come through nanostructuring: by engineering materials at the nanometer scale, researchers have been able to disrupt the pathways through which heat propagates through a lattice without correspondingly impeding electron flow, effectively decoupling thermal and electrical conductivity in ways that bulk materials do not permit. Nanostructured bismuth telluride composites, silicon-germanium alloys, and half-Heusler compounds have each achieved ZT values significantly above two in laboratory settings, representing a qualitative shift in what thermoelectric technology can theoretically deliver. [3]", "Despite these laboratory advances, the translation of high-ZT materials into deployable commercial systems remains a formidable engineering challenge. Many of the materials achieving the highest ZT values involve rare, expensive, or environmentally hazardous elements — tellurium, in particular, is both scarce and difficult to extract sustainably. The scalable fabrication of nanostructured materials without degrading the properties that make them effective at the nanoscale has proved technically demanding. Thermoelectric modules must also operate reliably across wide temperature ranges and extended lifetimes in environments that may be chemically aggressive or mechanically demanding. The integration of thermoelectric systems into existing industrial infrastructure requires careful thermal interface engineering — the management of heat flow at the junction between the heat source, the thermoelectric module, and the heat sink — which introduces further performance losses that laboratory measurements do not fully capture. [4]", "The sectors in which thermoelectric generation is most likely to achieve significant near-term deployment are those in which the combination of waste heat availability, operational environment, and tolerance for higher unit costs aligns most favorably with the technology's current capabilities. Space exploration has employed thermoelectric generators for decades, powering deep-space probes with the heat produced by radioactive decay in environments where solar panels are impractical. Remote industrial monitoring, military applications, and wearable medical devices represent additional niches where the absence of moving parts, long operational life, and the ability to harvest ambient thermal gradients outweigh the efficiency limitations that make thermoelectric generation uncompetitive in grid-scale power production.", "The longer-term trajectory of thermoelectric energy harvesting will be determined by whether advances in materials science can close the gap between laboratory performance and manufacturable, cost-effective systems. The emergence of machine learning as a tool for accelerating materials discovery — enabling researchers to screen millions of candidate compounds computationally before synthesizing the most promising in the laboratory — has introduced a new velocity to the search for high-performance thermoelectric materials that was not available to previous generations of researchers. Whether this acceleration proves sufficient to position thermoelectrics as a meaningful contributor to the broader energy transition, or whether the technology remains perpetually confined to high-value niche applications, is a question whose answer will depend as much on economics and manufacturing innovation as on the scientific principles that have defined the field since Seebeck's original observation two centuries ago."], questions: [{ q: "Which of the following best describes the central argument of the passage?", options: ["The future of thermoelectric technology depends entirely on whether machine learning can identify new materials that eliminate the need for rare and hazardous elements.", "Thermoelectric generation is a mature and commercially viable technology that is being underutilized due to lack of political will and investment.", "Thermoelectric generation holds significant theoretical promise for recovering industrial waste heat, but its widespread deployment is constrained by unresolved material, engineering, and economic challenges.", "Recent breakthroughs in nanostructured materials have made thermoelectric generation competitive with solar and wind power for grid-scale electricity production."], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"address\" in paragraph 1 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["effectively tackle and make progress in overcoming", "temporarily work around without resolving permanently", "publicly acknowledge and draw attention to", "formally identify and catalogue for future research"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following correctly describes one of the three properties required of an effective thermoelectric material?", options: ["It must exhibit a high Seebeck coefficient so that a given temperature difference produces a large voltage differential.", "It must be chemically inert so that it can operate without degradation in corrosive industrial environments.", "It must have high thermal conductivity to ensure heat flows rapidly and evenly across the material.", "It must have low electrical conductivity to prevent energy losses caused by excessive current flow through the device."], answer: 0 }, { q: "What does the author imply in paragraph 4 about the significance of nanostructuring for thermoelectric materials?", options: ["By engineering materials at the nanoscale, researchers have been able to reduce thermal conductivity without proportionally reducing electrical conductivity, substantially raising achievable ZT values.", "Nanostructuring has proven most effective when applied to metals rather than semiconductors, reversing the historical assumption that semiconductors were better thermoelectric candidates.", "Nanostructuring has allowed researchers to eliminate the need for the Seebeck coefficient as the primary measure of thermoelectric performance.", "Nanostructuring enables researchers to increase electrical conductivity in bulk materials without requiring rare or expensive elemental compositions."], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"conflicting\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["rarely occurring together in the same class of materials", "technically complex and difficult to measure accurately", "recently discovered and not yet fully understood by researchers", "mutually incompatible and working against each other"], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a nanostructured material that has achieved high ZT values in laboratory settings?", options: ["Silicon-germanium alloys", "Half-Heusler compounds", "Bismuth telluride composites", "Copper selenide nanocrystals"], answer: 3 }, { q: "What does the author imply in paragraph 5 about the relationship between laboratory ZT values and real-world thermoelectric performance?", options: ["Real-world deployment introduces engineering losses — particularly at thermal interfaces and across operating lifetimes — that laboratory measurements do not fully account for, creating a gap between theoretical and practical performance.", "The ZT values achieved in laboratory settings can be preserved in commercial systems provided that manufacturers invest in sufficiently advanced fabrication equipment.", "Performance losses in deployed systems are primarily caused by the chemical hazards associated with tellurium, which degrades thermoelectric modules over time.", "Laboratory ZT measurements are deliberately inflated by researchers to attract funding, and actual deployed performance is invariably lower."], answer: 0 }, { q: "By combining information from paragraphs 5 and 6, what conclusion can be drawn about why thermoelectric generation has succeeded in space exploration but remained limited in grid-scale power production?", options: ["Space applications use more advanced thermoelectric materials than are available for commercial use because defense and space agencies classify the most effective compounds.", "Space applications tolerate higher unit costs, require no moving parts, and operate in environments where the technology's current efficiency limitations are acceptable, whereas grid-scale production demands cost and efficiency levels the technology has not yet achieved.", "The radioactive decay heat sources used in space thermoelectric generators produce a form of thermal energy that is chemically incompatible with the industrial waste heat available for grid-scale applications.", "Space probes operate in environments with extreme and stable temperature gradients that maximize thermoelectric output, whereas grid-scale applications require more uniform heat distribution."], answer: 1 }, { q: "Which of the following best characterizes the author's attitude toward the future commercial prospects of thermoelectric energy?", options: ["Frustrated, as the author implies that insufficient government funding has prevented a technology with proven commercial viability from reaching its potential.", "Guardedly analytical, presenting the technology's potential and obstacles evenhandedly while noting that the outcome depends on interacting scientific, economic, and manufacturing factors.", "Dismissive, as the author concludes that thermoelectric generation will always be confined to niche applications regardless of future material advances.", "Enthusiastic, as the author believes recent nanotechnology breakthroughs have made large-scale thermoelectric deployment imminent."], answer: 1 }, { q: "Which of the following square brackets [1] , [2] , [3] , or [4] best indicates where the following sentence could be added to the passage? \"This absence of mechanical complexity gives thermoelectric devices an inherent reliability advantage over turbine-based generation systems, which require regular maintenance and are vulnerable to mechanical failure under continuous operation.\"", options: ["[1]", "[2]", "[3]", "[4]"], answer: 0 }] },
  ] },
];
